nd offering possible future opportunities. Whereas *J11* would block the line, but is far away from my stones and offers no potential for me. I consider that F7 can lead to my stones connecting. Even with me playing F7 to block, the White can still force me to respond.\n\nI think about whether White can create a "4-3" or "4-4" on the next move if I do play F7 or J11. But I find that it is very unlikely. If I play F7, White *must* play at J11 or play at some other location, and if I play J11, white must play at F7. White can be forced to play by making the move for black an immediate win or create a situation where white must make one of these blocks. After considering all possible countermoves, I conclude that in order for Black to have a chance of winning or for black to not lose immediately, *I must block the diagonal with F7*.\n\nSo, my final choice is F7, which becomes the evaluation, reason, and answer.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state presents a critical defensive situation for Black. White has formed a diagonal line of three stones at G8, H9, and I10. This configuration is highly dangerous because if White is allowed to play at either F7 or J11, they will create an "Open 4" (a line of four stones with both ends open), which leads to an unstoppable win. Black has no immediate winning combination (VCF) to ignore this threat; the potential "Sleep 4" at K5 (connecting J6-I7-H8) can be easily blocked by White at L4 without removing White\'s main threat. Therefore, Black\'s only viable option is to block White\'s diagonal line.\n\nReason: White threatens to create a winning "Open 4" by playing at F7 or J11, extending the diagonal chain G8-H9-I10. Black must play at one of these coordinates to prevent immediate defeat. F7 is the superior defensive choice compared to J11 because it is located centrally and adjacent to Black\'s existing stone cluster (G6, G7, H8). Playing F7 not only blocks the opponent but also maintains connection and influence