mediate win on the next turn with D11, creating my live four is, unfortunately, irrelevant at this stage.\n\nChecking the board again, and re-verifying the diagonal, I see the positions are: H7(O), G8(O), F9(O), and E10(O). D11 is open. The coordinates for this must be 8,7, 7,8, 6,9, 5,10, and 4,11 (D11). The I6 position is indeed a block.\n\nThe strategy here is simple. My analysis shows that I must block White\'s diagonal 4. This is the only move. I must play at D11. No other option.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board situation is critical for Black. While Black has a strong shape with a "Live 3" on the diagonal at G7-H8-I9 (which could become a "Live 4" and lead to a win), the opponent (White) has an immediate lethal threat. White has connected four stones diagonally at H7, G8, F9, and E10. This formation is blocked at the top-right by Black\'s stone at I6, but it is open at the bottom-left at D11.\n\nReason: White\'s diagonal line (H7-G8-F9-E10) forms a "Four". If Black does not block this immediately, White will play at D11 on their next turn to create a 5-in-a-row and win the game. In Gomoku, preventing an immediate 5-in-a-row takes precedence over any of your own attacks unless you can win on the very same turn (which Black cannot do here, as creating a 4 allows White to win first). Therefore, blocking at D11 is the only viable move to prolong the game.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "D", "row": 11 }'}
