 the lower-right end at K12. This formation is a "Four" (or "4-in-a-row"), which means White can play at K12 on their next turn to complete a "Five" and win the game immediately.\n\nBlack has a vertical cluster of stones in column I (I6-I9), but this is blocked at both ends by White stones at I5 and I10, making it effectively dead. Black also has diagonal lines of three (e.g., G7-H8-I9), but these are blocked on one end (J10) and cannot be extended to a winning 5 faster than White\'s current threat.\n\nSince Black has no immediate winning move (VCF) to outpace White, Black must play defensively to block White\'s lethal threat.\n\nReason: White has a diagonal 4-in-a-row (G8, H9, I10, J11) open at K12. If Black does not play K12, White will play there next turn to form 5-in-a-row and win. Black is forced to block.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 12}'}
