al move at G10 is a bigger threat.\n\nOkay, let\'s look at White\'s threats more closely. They have the "split four" in column G (G8, G9, G11, G12). If White plays G10, it\'s an immediate win. This is the critical threat!\n\nNow the question is, can I win *immediately*? Is there a move that gives me 5 in a row that I missed? Not looking like it. Let me try looking at the most dense collection of stones.\n\nIf Black has a move to make 5 in a row, it overrides all other concerns. So let\'s look for a "4" to become a "5".\n*   Row 8.\n*   H8, I8, J8 (3 in a row).\n*   Play K8 (4 in a row - forces White to play L8)\n*   Play F6, K5...\n    *   These moves create four and force White to block. However, they don\'t immediately win.\n    *   Does Black have a "Four-Three" or a "Four-Four"?\n    *   Let\'s check J7.\n    *   J7 creates a 3. J7(X) J6(X) J8(X) - which is blocked at J9.\n    *   If I place J7, I threaten to play J5. Is that 4? No.\n\nLet\'s double check White.\n*   White can immediately win if they make 5 in a row at G10.\n\nDoes Black have any winning move that creates a "Five"? No!\n\n*   Therefore, the only valid move for Black is G10. It is a necessary block.\n    *   White cannot win.\n    *   Black has no chance to win this turn.\n\nI can\'t allow that. I have to block White at G10. There\'s no way around it. G10 it is! So my move is G10, and here\'s the summary.\n\n**Therefore, the only move to prevent an immediate loss is to play at G10. This blocks White\'s potential five-in-a-row in column G and forces the game to continue.**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current board state presents a critical situation for Black.\n1.  **White\'s Threat:** White has stones at G8, G9, G11, and G12. This forms a split line of four stones in Column G. If White plays at G10, they will connect G8-G12 for a 5-in-a-row, winning the game immediately. This is a lethal "Mate in 1" threat.\n2.  **Black\'s Offensive Options:**\n    *   **K8:** Black has three stones at H8, I8, J8. Playing K8 would form a "Four" (H8-K8), forcing White to block at L8. However, this is not an immediate win (it requires a second turn). White would simply ignore the threat (or block if forced rules apply, but usually 5 wins instantly) or, more accurately, since it\'s Black\'s turn, Black plays K8, threatens next turn win. White\'s turn -> White plays G10 and wins. Black effectively loses the race.\n    *   **F6:** Black has a diagonal line G7-H8-I9. Playing F6 creates a "Four" (F6-I9). Similar to K8, this threatens a win on the subsequent turn, but White will win at G10 on their turn.\n    *   **K5:** Extends diagonal J6-I7-H8 to a "Four". Same outcome; too slow.\n3.  **Black\'s Immediate Win:** Black has a vertical line I6-I9 (4 stones), but it is blocked by O at I5 and I10. Black has no move to create 5-in-a-row immediately.\n\nReason:\nSince Black cannot win immediately and White has a move (G10) that secures an immediate win, Black\'s only valid option is to block White\'s winning line. Playing at G10 prevents White from forming 5-in-a-row and prolongs the game.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 10}'}
